      <h1>Capacity Rates: C20, C10 and C5</h1>
      <p class="muted">Battery Testing Methods · Last reviewed 2026-09-16</p>

      <div class="box">
        <strong>Direct answer:</strong> A battery's measured capacity depends on <strong>how fast it is
        discharged</strong>. The rate is written as <strong>C20</strong> (the current that drains the battery in 20
        hours), <strong>C10</strong> (10 hours) or <strong>C5</strong> (5 hours). Faster discharge gives a
        <strong>lower</strong> measured capacity, so a capacity figure is meaningless without its rate.
      </div>

      <h2>The rate ladder</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Rate</th><th>Discharge time</th><th>Measured capacity</th><th>Typical use</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>C20</strong></td><td>20 hours</td><td>Highest (the quoted Ah)</td><td>General rating, solar</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>C10</strong></td><td>10 hours</td><td>Lower than C20</td><td>Industrial / telecom</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>C5</strong></td><td>5 hours</td><td>Lower again</td><td>UPS, high-rate applications</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>Why faster discharge lowers capacity</h2>
      <p>
        At higher currents, more of the stored energy is wasted inside the battery — as heat in the internal
        resistance and through slower electrochemical access to the active material. A battery that delivers 100 Ah
        at the gentle C20 rate may deliver only ~85 Ah at C10 and less still at C5. The rate is part of the
        specification, not a footnote — see <a href="/capacity-testing/">capacity testing</a> for the full C20 method.
      </p>

      <h2>How to compare two capacity claims</h2>
      <p>
        Compare <strong>within the same rate</strong>: C20 to C20, C10 to C10. A battery quoted at C10 will look
        smaller than one quoted at C20 even if it is physically similar. The same discipline applies as with
        <a href="/cca-testing/">CCA standards</a> — the number's basis defines what it means.
      </p>

      <h2>The Author's Take</h2>
      <div class="box">
        <p><strong>Position:</strong> Capacity is not one number — it is a curve over discharge rate, and the single
        most useful question about any Ah figure is "at what rate?", because the answer changes the number.</p>
        <p><strong>Reasoning:</strong> The C20 number is the flattering slow rate; real applications discharge faster and
        get less. A buyer who compares within one rate — and knows the C20/C10/C5 ladder — reads capacity claims the
        way engineers do.</p>
        <p class="muted"><em>This is the author's editorial view, not a purchasing guarantee.</em></p>
      </div>

